1. The Error Collection (Named Exception Species)
Poka-yoke translates to mistake-proofing—building error handling into programs so invalid inputs do not cause sudden crashes.
| Exception Species | What It Really Means | Where You Met It / Example |
|---|---|---|
| TypeError | Two incompatible types (e.g., adding 25 + "30"). | Job 08 |
| ValueError | Right type, but hopeless input (e.g., float("twentyfive")). | Work Order 03 |
| NameError | Variable or function used before declaration. | Job 10 |
| KeyError | Looking up a non-existent key in a dictionary. | Job 16 |
| IndexError | Accessing a list position beyond its range. | Work Order 05 |
| AttributeError | Method or property does not exist on that object (e.g., P.make_part()). | Job 22 |
| ZeroDivisionError | Attempting to divide by zero (e.g., average of an empty batch). | Job 23 |
> Reading Tracebacks: Read the last line first—that identifies the error species. Then check the line number to locate where the fault occurred.
