2. The Structure of a Guard (try / except / else / finally)
try:
    # Only the risky line goes here
    dia = float(input("Diameter: "))[span_17](start_span)[span_17](end_span)
except ValueError:
    # Name the expected exception and give clear feedback/recovery
    print("Not a number - enter e.g. 25.02")[span_18](start_span)[span_18](end_span)
    dia = None[span_19](start_span)[span_19](end_span)
else:
    # Executes ONLY if NO error occurred in the try block
    print("Reading accepted")[span_20](start_span)[span_20](end_span)
finally:
    # ALWAYS executes regardless of whether an error occurred or not (cleanup)
    print("Gauge released")[span_21](start_span)[span_21](end_span)

3. The Two Rules of Good Guards
 * Keep the try Block Short: Place only the one or two risky lines inside the try block. Wrapping dozens of lines inside try makes it difficult to pinpoint which line failed.
 * Name the Exception Species Specifically: Always specify the exact error you anticipate handling (e.g., except ValueError:).
4. The Bare-Except Trap
 * What it is: Writing a plain except: without specifying an exception class.
 * Why it's dangerous: A bare except: catches all errors—including typos, system interrupts, and logic mistakes. It hides underlying bugs permanently instead of resolving them safely.
> Key Rule: A guard that hides problems is worse than no guard at all. Always name the species you expect.
